    h1: "How Is a Seattle Building Retrofitted for Earthquakes?",
    answer: "The engineering answer is that a Seattle seismic retrofit is a targeted surgery on the building's weakest links, not a rebuild of the whole structure. Most of the city's vulnerable stock is unreinforced masonry (URM) — brick buildings from the early 1900s whose walls were never tied to the floors — plus older concrete and wood-frame buildings with soft first stories. A retrofit designed to current practice starts with an ASCE 41 seismic evaluation that grades the building's expected performance, then adds exactly the strength, anchorage, and continuity the evaluation says is missing. Direct answer: the engineer evaluates the building against ASCE 41, anchors masonry walls to each floor and the roof for out-of-plane forces, braces or removes hazardous parapets, strengthens weak diaphragms, and fixes soft-story or weak-story irregularities — all permitted through the Seattle Department of Construction and Inspections with structural observation during construction.\n\nSeattle sits in one of the highest seismic hazard zones in the country, with the Cascadia subduction zone offshore and the Seattle fault running directly beneath the city. That hazard is why the city has pursued a mandatory URM retrofit policy: owners of qualifying brick buildings must bring them up to defined retrofit standards on a compliance timeline. The engineering work therefore has a regulatory shape as well as a technical one. The structural engineer determines which compliance tier the building falls into, scopes the retrofit to that tier, and produces drawings that the plan reviewer can check against the city's published retrofit requirements. Buildings in unincorporated King County follow the county's permitting path instead, and while the technical work is the same, the submittal portal, review timelines, and inspection scheduling differ — the engineer confirms the authority having jurisdiction before the evaluation begins.\n\nWall anchorage is the heart of nearly every URM retrofit. In an earthquake, an unanchored brick wall tries to peel away from the building and fall outward — the classic, deadly failure seen in every major masonry earthquake. The retrofit installs through-bolts or adhesive anchors connecting each wall to the floor and roof diaphragms at regular spacing, with steel plates or channels on the exterior face spreading the load across the masonry. The engineer sizes each anchor for the out-of-plane force the code's seismic provisions assign to wall elements, checks that the diaphragm itself can deliver that force to the lateral system, and details the connection so a brittle masonry failure cannot precede the anchor's yield. Parapets get the same treatment or are cut down: an unbraced brick parapet above the roofline is essentially a freestanding wall waiting for a reason to fall, so the engineer either braces it back to the roof structure or removes it.\n\nDiaphragms and vertical irregularities come next. Many older Seattle buildings have wood floors that act as flexible diaphragms, and the retrofit often needs plywood overlay shear transfer, new collectors, or added shear walls to create a complete load path from the walls to the foundation. Soft stories — open retail or parking at grade with housing above — get steel moment frames or strengthened walls at the open level. Throughout, the engineer designs for the building's actual materials: century-old brick has far lower reliable strength than modern masonry, so the evaluation uses material testing or conservative default properties rather than assuming modern capacities. The retrofit never promises the building will be undamaged in a major earthquake; it is engineered so the building does not collapse and occupants can exit safely.",
    directAnswer: "A Seattle building is retrofitted by evaluating it under ASCE 41, anchoring unreinforced masonry walls to floors and roofs for out-of-plane forces, bracing or removing parapets, strengthening diaphragms, and correcting soft stories — permitted through Seattle's building department or King County for unincorporated sites.",
