    title: "How Is a Rain Screen Wall Assembly Designed for Portland?",
    description: "Portland rain screen design uses pressure-equalized cavities, continuous drainage planes, and meticulous window flashing to shed wind-driven rain off the building envelope.",
    h1: "How Is a Rain Screen Wall Assembly Designed for Portland?",
    answer: "The engineering answer is that a rain screen is a two-stage defense: an outer cladding that takes the beating from wind-driven rain, and a drained, ventilated cavity behind it that catches whatever gets through and sends it harmlessly back outside. Portland's climate — months of wind-driven rain, not just vertical rainfall — makes the cavity's pressure equalization the critical detail. When the air pressure in the cavity matches the pressure outside the cladding, wind has no force left to push water inward through the joints. Direct answer: the engineer designs the cladding as a vented outer skin, sizes a continuous drainage cavity with vents top and bottom, specifies a fully sealed air and water barrier on the sheathing as the true weather line, and details every window, penetration, and base flashing so water entering the cavity drains out instead of reaching the structure.\n\nPortland's rain does not fall straight down. Winter storms drive rain horizontally against walls for weeks, and any cladding system that relies on face-sealed joints — caulk as the only defense — will eventually leak as sealant ages and joints move. The rain screen principle accepts that the outer cladding will leak a little and engineers the assembly so that leakage does not matter. The cavity, typically three-quarters of an inch to two inches deep, breaks capillary action, and compartmentalization within the cavity keeps pressure equalized across each section rather than letting wind pressurize the whole wall. The engineer lays out the compartment divisions on the elevations so the installer builds them where drawn, not where convenient.\n\nThe air and water barrier on the sheathing is the assembly's real weather line, and its continuity is the engineer's obsession. Every sheet joint, every fastener penetration, every window rough opening must be sealed into one continuous plane. Window detailing is where rain screens most often fail: the engineer specifies pan flashing with end dams at sills, jamb flashing integrated shingle-fashion with the barrier, and head flashing that kicks water out over the cladding below. At the base of the wall, through-wall flashing with weep vents drains the cavity to the exterior, and the engineer keeps that drainage path clear of mortar droppings, insulation squeeze-out, and sealant — the field failures are almost always blocked drainage, not bad theory.\n\nMaterial selection follows the exposure. In Portland's wet, moderate climate, the engineer favors cladding and furring that tolerate constant dampness: fiber cement, metal panels, or treated wood on non-corrosive furring, with stainless or hot-dipped galvanized fasteners. Where the rain screen goes over mass timber or wood framing, the cavity's ventilation dries the assembly between storms, which is exactly why the vents must stay open and the cavity must stay clear. The engineer also coordinates the rain screen with the building's airtightness strategy, because the same barrier plane that stops water typically serves as the air barrier — one continuous, tested plane doing two jobs.",
