        body: "Pressure equalization is the physics that makes a rain screen work in wind-driven rain. Wind pressing on the cladding face creates a pressure difference that would drive water through any joint; venting the cavity to the exterior lets cavity pressure rise to match, eliminating the driving force. The engineer sizes vent openings to balance two competing needs: enough open area to equalize pressure quickly during gusts, but baffled or compartmented so wind does not blow straight through and pressurize the cavity unevenly. Horizontal and vertical compartment seals divide the cavity into zones, each vented independently, so a gust on one elevation does not drive cavity air — and water — sideways into a sheltered zone.\n\nCavity depth and drainage capacity are engineered, not guessed. The depth must break capillary bridging between cladding and barrier, provide room for the expected volume of incidental water to drain down the barrier face, and accommodate cladding movement and installation tolerance. The engineer details the cavity base with a sloped through-flashing and weep vents at regular spacing, and specifies insect screening that keeps pests out without choking airflow. At floor lines and shelf angles, the cavity must continue uninterrupted or be flashed across with the same drainage logic — a shelf angle that dams the cavity is a leak manufactured into the drawings.",
