    h1: "How Is MEP Designed for a Mass Timber Office in the Pacific Northwest?",
    answer: "The engineering answer is that a mass timber office flips the normal MEP workflow: the beautiful exposed wood ceiling the architect is selling is also the surface every duct, sprinkler pipe, light fixture, and conduit must attach to, with no plenum to hide mistakes. Coordination stops being a background task and becomes the central design discipline. The engineer routes systems in a shallow zone beneath the timber deck, keeps every penetration through the timber planned and sleeved, and protects the wood's fire and acoustic performance at each crossing. Direct answer: the engineer establishes a tight MEP zone under the exposed CLT or glulam deck, coordinates all trades in a shared model before construction, routes sprinkler and duct mains in that zone with drops only where needed, and details every timber penetration with the fire-resistance and acoustic performance the assembly requires.\n\nThe exposed ceiling drives every routing decision. In a conventional office, ducts disappear above a drop ceiling; in a mass timber office, the structure is the finish, so ductwork, cable tray, and sprinkler piping run exposed in the zone between the timber deck and the top of the finished space. The engineer works with the architect to set that zone's depth — often 18 to 30 inches — and then treats it as precious: mains run in coordinated corridors, branch ducts drop only to serve diffusers, and lighting integrates with the structure rather than fighting it. Sprinkler piping, which cannot be value-engineered out of sight, is routed with the same care as the ductwork, because black steel pipe against warm wood is a visual the client will live with for decades.\n\nPenetrations through mass timber are engineered, not field-cut. Every duct, pipe, and conduit crossing a CLT floor panel or glulam beam needs a planned opening: the structural engineer sizes and locates it so the timber's capacity and fire performance survive, and the MEP engineer routes to those openings rather than inventing new ones. Fire stopping at timber penetrations uses systems tested for the char behavior of mass timber, and the engineer verifies that recessed lights, speakers, and other devices mounted in or near the timber do not create heat or ignition concerns. Acoustic performance needs the same attention: a CLT floor that looks monolithic can still transmit footfall and voice between offices, so the engineer coordinates floor buildups, resilient layers, and sealant at every penetration to hold the acoustic rating.\n\nMoisture and condensation discipline matters in the Pacific Northwest's damp climate. Supply ducts running in the exposed zone can sweat if the air is cold and the space is humid, so the engineer insulates and vapor-seals ductwork that carries cold air, and checks that the timber deck above does not become a condensing surface where warm, moist return air meets cold structure. Commissioning is heavier than in a conventional office because nothing can be adjusted after finishes: the engineer verifies air balance, controls sequences, and lighting scenes before the space is occupied, since opening an exposed timber ceiling to fix a damper is not an option anyone wants.",
