    h1: "How Are Schools Engineered for the Cascade Foothills?",
    answer: "The engineering answer is that a Cascade foothill school is designed for two dominant hazards at once: the Cascadia subduction zone and regional crustal faults driving high seismic demands, and elevation-driven snow loads that can dwarf the gravity loads the building would see in the lowlands. The structural engineer sizes the lateral system for the seismic design category the site's hazard demands, sizes roofs for the site-specific snow load including drifts, and makes the big-volume spaces — gymnasiums, cafeterias, multipurpose rooms — work as both daily school space and post-disaster community shelter. Direct answer: the engineer designs the lateral system for the site's seismic design category with ductile detailing, roofs for elevation-specific snow plus drift and sliding loads, long-span gym and cafeteria structures as robust diaphragms, and egress, emergency power, and water systems so the school can serve as a community shelter.\n\nSeismic design in the foothills starts with the site hazard. Ground shaking intensifies with proximity to the subduction zone and with local soil conditions — soft valley soils amplify shaking compared to foothill rock — so the engineer obtains a geotechnical investigation that characterizes the site class and any liquefaction or slope-stability concerns before the structural system is chosen. School occupancies carry a higher importance factor than ordinary buildings because of their occupant load and their post-earthquake role, which raises the design forces across the board. The lateral system — typically concrete or masonry shear walls, or steel braced frames around the gym — is detailed for ductility so it can deform in a major earthquake without collapse, and nonstructural components get full seismic bracing: ceilings, lights, mechanical equipment, and bookcases are all engineered not to become hazards over occupied classrooms.\n\nSnow is the gravity counterpart. At foothill elevations, ground snow loads climb steeply, and the roof design must handle balanced snow, unbalanced drift against parapets and rooftop units, and sliding snow from upper roofs onto lower ones — a common foothill condition where a two-story classroom wing sheds onto a single-story connector. The engineer checks each drift condition the code's snow provisions describe, because drifts, not the uniform load, usually govern the design of the most heavily loaded members. Roof drainage is designed so a midwinter thaw does not pond water behind snow-blocked drains; overflow scuppers sized for the rain-on-snow event are cheap insurance the engineer always includes.\n\nThe gymnasium deserves its own engineering story. It is the longest span in the building, the largest open diaphragm, and the space most likely to serve as an emergency shelter — which means the engineer designs its roof structure for the combined snow-plus-shelter occupancy, its walls for the out-of-plane forces of a tall volume, and its systems for shelter operation: emergency power for lighting and communications, domestic water storage or well backup, and ventilation that works when the grid is down. Egress from the gym is sized for the shelter occupant load, not just the school day.",
