    description: "Spokane warehouse design sizes roofs for high ground snow loads, drift against parapets and rooftop units, and ponding stability on long flat spans.",
    h1: "How Are Spokane Warehouses Designed for Heavy Snow?",
    answer: "The engineering answer is that a Spokane warehouse roof is designed for snow as the controlling load case, full stop. Eastern Washington's ground snow loads are among the highest in the state, and on a 200-foot clear-span warehouse roof, snow — not wind, not seismic — sizes the structure. The engineer converts the site's ground snow load into roof design loads, checks every drift and unbalanced condition the long flat roof creates, and verifies ponding stability so a clogged drain during a thaw cannot trigger a progressive collapse. Direct answer: the engineer designs the roof framing for the site ground snow load with drift, unbalanced, and sliding checks at every parapet, step, and rooftop unit, verifies ponding stability of the flexible long-span roof, and details drainage with overflow scuppers so blocked primary drains never pond the roof.\n\nGround snow load is the starting number, and in the Spokane area it is high enough that the engineer treats the geotechnical and snow data with equal seriousness. The ground snow load converts to a flat-roof snow load through exposure, thermal, and importance factors — a heated warehouse melts some snow from beneath, an unheated storage bay does not — and then the real work begins with the non-uniform loads. Parapets, which every warehouse has, create leeward drifts that can pile snow several feet deep in a wedge against the wall; rooftop units create their own drifts on both sides; and any step in roof elevation, where a high office section meets the low warehouse roof, collects sliding and drifted snow from above. The engineer checks each of these against the capacity of the joists, girders, and columns below, because the drift wedge — not the uniform blanket — almost always governs the heaviest-loaded members.\n\nPonding is the silent killer of flat warehouse roofs. Long-span joist roofs are flexible, and flexibility plus water equals a feedback loop: water ponds in the deflected low spot, the added weight deflects the roof further, which ponds more water. The engineer runs a ponding stability analysis that proves the roof is stiff enough to arrest this cycle under the rain-on-snow event — the midwinter condition where rain falls on the snowpack and drains must pass the combined flow. Primary drains are sized for that event, and overflow scuppers through the parapet are set just above the primary drain inlet elevation so they engage before ponding depth becomes dangerous. The engineer also specifies the roof slope honestly: a nominal quarter-inch-per-foot that deflects flat under snow is not drainage, and tapered insulation or structural slope is designed to keep water moving.\n\nThe lateral system gets its own snow-aware design. Seismic forces in Spokane are moderate, but the seismic weight of the building includes a prescribed portion of the snow load — a warehouse carrying a heavy snowpack is a heavier building in an earthquake, and the engineer includes that mass in the lateral analysis. Braced frames or tilt-up concrete shear walls, the two common warehouse lateral systems, are sized for the combined demand, and the roof diaphragm — usually steel deck — is designed to drag the seismic forces into those walls while also handling the snow-gravity deflections without distress.",
    directAnswer: "Spokane warehouses are designed with snow as the controlling load: roofs sized for the site ground snow load plus drift, unbalanced, and sliding checks at every parapet and rooftop unit, ponding stability verified for the flexible long-span roof, and overflow drainage so blocked drains never pond the roof during a thaw.",
