        body: "The engineer lays out the zones before any equipment is selected. Each water zone gets its own booster pump arrangement — typically duplex or triplex pumps with variable-speed drives — drawing from a break tank that also provides the code-required storage and backflow separation. The zones cascade: the lowest zone may feed directly from street pressure through reducing valves, while upper zones boost from the break tanks. The engineer models the full-day demand profile so pumps are not short-cycling at night or starving the top floor during the morning peak. Hot water follows the same zoning, with per-zone heaters or heat exchangers keeping distribution loops compact.\n\nHVAC distribution respects the same vertical logic. Central plants in the basement or on mechanical floors serve zones of manageable height; beyond that, static pressure and duct leakage make central air distribution impractical. The engineer often pairs a central heating/cooling plant with floor-by-floor air handlers or fan-coil systems, which also gives tenants the after-hours control a Bellevue office market expects. Shaft space is the currency: the engineer reserves riser closets and horizontal distribution corridors early with the architect, because a high-rise that runs out of shaft space runs out of options.",
