    h1: "How Is a Port Logistics Facility in Tacoma Engineered?",
    answer: "The engineering answer is that a port logistics facility is heavy civil engineering wearing an industrial site plan: pavements designed for fully loaded container handlers rather than cars, drainage sized for acres of impervious yard, electrical distribution feeding hundreds of refrigerated container plugs, and structures detailed for the seismic hazard of a waterfront fill site. The engineer designs the yard as a machine for moving boxes, with every system sized for the peak the terminal operator actually runs. Direct answer: the engineer designs rigid or heavy-duty flexible pavements for container-handling equipment loads, grades and drains the yard through oil-water separation and treatment, distributes medium-voltage power to reefer racks and yard lighting, and engineers warehouses, canopies, and gate structures for seismic and wind loads on liquefiable waterfront soils.\n\nPavement is the facility's structural system. A loaded reach stacker or top-pick can put axle loads an order of magnitude beyond highway trucks, and it turns, brakes, and stacks in the same lanes thousands of times. The engineer designs the pavement section — typically portland cement concrete or deep-strength asphalt — for the equipment's actual wheel loads and repetition counts, with a subgrade investigation that characterizes the fill and marine sediments common along the Tacoma waterfront. Concrete joint layout is engineered for the panel sizes the loads demand, and the engineer details the joints and sealants for the constant trafficking; a pavement that faults or spalls under the handlers becomes a maintenance crisis within years. Drainage inlets are set in the pavement with frames rated for the equipment, because a crushed inlet grate in a container lane is a safety hazard.\n\nStormwater on a multi-acre impervious yard is a quantity and quality problem together. The engineer grades the yard to inlets at slopes that move water without ponding in the travel lanes, then routes the flow through oil-water separators and treatment sized for the vehicle and equipment pollutants a working terminal generates — drips, hydraulic leaks, tire wear. Tacoma's stormwater requirements, shaped by the Puget Sound setting, demand treatment before discharge, and the engineer designs the treatment train — separators, biofiltration or media filters, detention — to fit a site where every square foot has operational value. Spill containment at fueling and maintenance areas is designed as a separate, isolated system.\n\nPower distribution is industrial in scale. Reefer racks — the plugged-in refrigerated containers — draw continuous significant load each, and a terminal with hundreds of plugs needs a medium-voltage distribution loop with unit substations placed to minimize voltage drop. The engineer sizes transformers for the diversified reefer load plus yard lighting, gate systems, and warehouse loads, with metering that lets the operator bill shipping lines for plug power. High-mast lighting is engineered for the illumination the terminal's nighttime operations require while controlling glare and light spill toward the water and neighboring uses. Seismic design overlays everything: waterfront fills can liquefy, so the engineer follows the geotechnical recommendations for ground improvement or deep foundations, and details yard structures — light poles, canopies, gate booths, racking — for the amplified shaking liquefiable soils produce.",
