        question: "Why are coastal wind loads so much higher than inland?",
        answer: "Two compounding factors: the basic wind speed contours are highest along the coast, and the exposure category for open water is the most severe — with no trees, buildings, or terrain to slow the wind, the full velocity reaches the building. The engineer multiplies these together into velocity pressures that can be multiples of what the same building would see inland.",
