        heading: "CO2 Safety, Drainage, and Wastewater Pretreatment",
        body: "CO2 protection is engineered in layers. The engineer designs mechanical ventilation for the cellar and tank areas sized to dilute the maximum credible CO2 release, places fixed gas detection at low level — CO2 is heavier than air — with audible and visible alarms, and interlocks detection with increased ventilation and personnel alerting. Any new pits, trenches, or below-grade spaces are evaluated as permit-required confined spaces, and the engineer designs the access, ventilation, and rescue provisions the classification demands. Operating procedures and signage are coordinated with the owner's safety plan, but the engineering provides the systems the procedures rely on.\n\nThe drainage and pretreatment design handles the brewery's hardest effluent. Trench drains with chemical-resistant grating collect washdown across the sloped production floor; the engineer sizes the drainage for hose flows and details the waterproofing at every penetration, because brewery floors leak at the details. The wastewater stream goes through the pretreatment the city requires — typically screening for solids, equalization to smooth the batch discharges, and pH adjustment for the caustic and acid cleaning cycles — and the engineer designs the pretreatment equipment, its controls, and its own drainage and ventilation. Sampling and metering points the city requires for compliance are built into the design, not added after the inspector asks.",
