        body: "The engineer starts with the design vessel and the operator's berthing practice. Berthing energy — a function of vessel mass, approach velocity, and angle — sizes the fender system, and the engineer selects fenders (cone, cell, or pile-supported) for the energy absorption the operations require, then designs the supporting structure for the fender reactions. Mooring loads from wind and current on the berthed vessel add sustained lateral demand on the dolphins and pier; the engineer combines these with wave loads on the piles themselves, calculated for the site's exposure — open Sound versus sheltered inlet changes everything. Scour around the piles is evaluated with the geotechnical engineer, and pile tips are set below the predicted scour depth.\n\nSeismic detailing treats the overwater structure as the high-risk element it is. The engineer designs the pile-to-pile-cap connections for ductile hinging, checks the deck diaphragm's ability to distribute seismic forces among the piles, and evaluates liquefaction and lateral spreading of the marine sediments — the failure mode that has destroyed waterfront structures in past earthquakes. Ground improvement or deep founding below the liquefiable layer follows the geotechnical recommendations. The engineer also designs the utility and bridge connections between the fixed shore and the moving overwater structure with the flexibility the seismic displacement demands.",
